Year = {2026},
Volume = {54},
Number = {5},
Month = {AUG 28},
Abstract = {This study identifies how political mechanisms drive opportunistic
   fiscal behavior depending on whether fiscal authority is centralized or
   decentralized. Drawing on two theoretical mechanisms-political budget
   cycles and electoral competition-we investigate how the dominant
   mechanism of opportunism varies across levels of fiscal authority. We
   test these mechanisms in the context of local tax cuts in South Korea,
   where both central and local governments can implement tax reductions.
   Our findings show strong support for the political budget cycle
   hypothesis when the central government determines local tax cuts, with
   tax reductions increasing significantly as elections approach. However,
   electoral competition does not influence central government tax policy,
   likely because national policies are uniform and cannot target
   individual localities. In contrast, local governments exhibit
   heterogeneity-political opportunism is more pronounced in electorally
   competitive districts. These results suggest that local fiscal autonomy
   can either constrain or amplify political opportunism, depending on
   electoral incentives.Related Articles Lee, Yunsoo. 2026. ``Untangling
   the Relationship Between Fiscal Decentralization and Citizen
   Participation: A Critical Revisit.{''} Politics \& Policy 54, no. 1:
   e70121. https://doi.org/10.1111/polp.70121.
